Linux's microcode reading of the AMD bench (a Ryzen 7 4800U, family 23 model
96 stepping 1) under that machine's Fedora, `7.2.4-ogc3.1.fc44.x86_64` with
`amd-ucode-firmware-20260810`, whose `microcode_amd_fam17h.bin` is the
container `kernel/microcode/amd-ucode/` pins, byte for byte. Taken over ssh as
the bench's unprivileged user by the orchestrator, as the tools printed it,
and recorded at https://github.com/ToyOSOrg/evidence/blob/9e5f88e5e5de5dadba86d6939a158c728752dec8/amd-bench-linux-microcode.txt
with its command:

  journalctl -k -b 0 --no-pager | grep -i microcode | sed -E "s/^.*kernel: //";
  grep -m1 "^microcode" /proc/cpuinfo; grep -m1 "cpu family" /proc/cpuinfo;
  grep -m1 "^model\s" /proc/cpuinfo; grep -m1 stepping /proc/cpuinfo;
  rpm -q amd-ucode-firmware; uname -r

  microcode.txt   its output: the revision the firmware ran and the one Linux
                  loaded early over it. `microcode_on_metal` in
                  `tests/toyos.rs` holds every CPU's `microcode:` line to it.
